<p>The Netherlands functions less as a final destination and more as India's gateway into the wider EU grape market. Rotterdam's port scale and cold-chain distribution infrastructure make it the practical landing point even when the ultimate buyer is in Germany, France or elsewhere — so Dutch import figures capture more than domestic Dutch demand alone. The scale is real regardless: the Netherlands took roughly 39% of India's total HS 080610 grape export value in 2023. India's export season is short and intense: roughly January through April, peaking in February-March, timed to fill the gap between the end of Southern Hemisphere supply and the start of Northern Hemisphere harvests. This guide is for Dutch and EU importers planning a season's programme.</p>

<!-- slot:facts -->

<p>The WITS/UN Comtrade dataset for calendar 2023 (table above) puts the Netherlands at USD 129.68 million of India's total USD 328.77 million in HS 080610 grape exports that year — roughly 39% of total value, by far the largest single destination, ahead of the United Kingdom (USD 26.3 million), the UAE (USD 25.0 million), Bangladesh (USD 21.5 million) and Russia (USD 21.4 million).</p>

<h2>Variety and specification</h2>
<p>Thompson Seedless is the export mainstay, with Flame Seedless and (seasonally) Crimson Seedless covering red-grape demand, and Sonaka — a Thompson selection — also widely available. Specify berry size (16-18mm or 18mm+) and bunch weight expectations (typically 300-500g); mixed sizing within a carton is a common quality complaint on under-specified orders. TSS (sweetness, measured in degrees Brix) typically runs 16-18° at export maturity — tell us if your market expects a specific sweetness profile.</p>

<h2>What actually determines arrival quality on this route</h2>
<p>Given the 18-24 day transit to Rotterdam — the longest we quote at real volume for a perishable — three things matter more here than almost anywhere else:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Pre-cooling speed.</strong> Field heat removed slowly costs shelf life that can't be recovered later, however well the container is subsequently managed.</li>
  <li><strong>SO2 pad calibration.</strong> Sulphur dioxide generator pads inside the carton suppress botrytis (grey mould) over the long sea transit. Too little and mould develops; too much and bleaching occurs. Packing needs to match the actual transit length.</li>
  <li><strong>Consistent 0-2°C storage.</strong> No margin for temperature excursions on a route this long.</li>
</ul>

<!-- slot:docs -->

<p>EU maximum residue limits are strict and actively enforced at the border, and pesticide residue is the single most common cause of Indian fresh produce rejection entering the EU. We strongly recommend agreeing pre-shipment residue testing before the goods ship rather than treating it as something to sort out if a problem arises. {{ site.complianceChecklistCta }}</p>
<p>Phytosanitary certificates for EU-bound plant products are registered through the EU's TRACES NT platform, using a CHED-PP (Common Health Entry Document for Plants and Plant Products) entry. This is typically managed on the importer's side, using the certificate documentation we provide — worth confirming the process with your importer before your first shipment if this is new territory for either side.</p>

<!-- price-behaviour:start -->
<h2>What actually determines the price you're quoted</h2>
<p>Grape pricing moves more within a single season than almost any other product we handle, and we don't quote a flat per-carton number without knowing your specification and timing. Five things matter most: where you are in the January-April season (early and late-season fruit price differently than peak February-March volume, driven by both quality and simple supply), berry size and bunch-weight grade (18mm+ commands a premium over 16-18mm), variety (Thompson Seedless, Flame Seedless and Crimson Seedless don't price identically — red-grape varieties often carry a different demand curve than green), packaging format (retail punnets with custom artwork cost more per tonne than bulk cartons), and prevailing INR/EUR exchange rates plus reefer freight rates to Rotterdam, which are a meaningfully larger share of landed cost for grapes than for most non-perishable exports because of the cold-chain requirement.</p>
<p>Quotes are issued FOB, CIF or CFR against your specific variety, berry-size grade, packaging format and delivery week — never as a flat seasonal-average rate. To get an accurate quote fast, tell us: variety preference, berry size grade, packaging (bulk cartons or retail punnets, with or without your artwork), quantity, and your target delivery week within the season.</p>
