<h2>What goes wrong on a first order, and how to avoid it</h2>
<p>The single biggest cause of quality complaints on this route traces back to pre-cooling, not anything that happens at sea. Grapes carry significant field heat straight after harvest, and if that heat isn't removed quickly — ideally within hours, in a proper forced-air pre-cooling facility — the fruit enters the container already behind on shelf life that no amount of correct in-transit temperature management can recover. We pre-cool at the packhouse before loading specifically to avoid this, and it's a fair question to ask any supplier directly: how long between harvest and pre-cooling, and what facility do they use?</p>
<p>The second common issue is SO2 pad miscalibration. Too little sulphur dioxide and botrytis (grey mould) develops over the 18-24 day transit; too much and the berries bleach, a cosmetic defect that still causes rejection at retail-grade inspection. Pad specification needs to match the actual transit length to your specific port, not a generic default — we calibrate to route, and would recommend confirming any supplier does the same rather than assuming a one-size pad works for every destination.</p>
<p>Third, and this is the one first-time EU/Dutch buyers most often underestimate: how frequently and how rigorously EU border inspection actually tests fresh produce for pesticide residue. It isn't a spot-check — MRL (maximum residue limit) testing is a routine, expected part of EU fresh-produce entry, and it is the single most common rejection cause for Indian produce entering the EU generally, not just grapes. Buyers who assume "we'll deal with it if it comes up" are the ones who get a shipment held or rejected at the border. Agree residue testing before the goods ship, not after a problem surfaces.</p>
<p>A smaller but real issue: berry drop and shatter from rough handling during stuffing or from inadequate dunnage/bracing inside the container — grapes are more physically fragile in transit than most fruit we ship, and container-loading technique matters more here than for a hardier product.</p>
